%\VignetteIndexEntry{Speed and accuracy comparison for deterministic QMC}
%\VignetteDepends{mvtnorm}
%\VignetteKeywords{multivariate normal distribution, benchmark}
%\VignettePackage{mvtnorm}
%\VignetteEngine{utils::Sweave}

\newcommand{\pkg}[1]{\textbf{#1}}
\newcommand{\code}[1]{\texttt{#1}}

\begin{document}

\title{Speed and Accuracy Comparison for \code{DeterministicQMC}}
\author{The \pkg{mvtnorm} authors}
\maketitle

<<setup, echo=FALSE>>=
library("mvtnorm")
@

\section{Purpose}

The default \pkg{mvtnorm} algorithm, \code{GenzBretz}, is randomized
quasi-Monte Carlo (QMC) \citep{Genz_Bretz_2002,Genz_Bretz_2009}.  Its
randomization is one reason it is fast and useful: the algorithm can combine
low-discrepancy integration points with a stochastic error assessment.  The new
\code{DeterministicQMC} algorithm removes that randomization, so the relevant
question is empirical as well as methodological: how much speed is traded for
deterministic reproducibility, and how close are the resulting probabilities on
representative problems?

This vignette compares eight choices:

\begin{itemize}
\item \code{GenzBretz()}: the current default, with its default stopping
tolerances.
\item \code{Miwa(steps = 128)}: the established deterministic low-dimensional
normal algorithm.
\item original randomized QMC: \code{GenzBretz(maxpts = 25000, abseps = 0,
releps = 0)}, which disables early stopping and uses the historical
Genz-Bretz QMC implementation at a fixed point budget.
\item \code{DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 25000, backend = "c")}: the new plain C
deterministic Halton backend for normal probabilities.
\item \code{DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 25000, backend = "sobol")}: the new
plain C deterministic Sobol backend for normal probabilities.
\item \code{DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 25000, backend = "hybrid")}: an
experimental Sobol backend with a deterministic tail-spaced change of
variables.
\item \code{DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 25000, backend = "genzsobol")}: an
experimental Sobol backend with variable reordering and embedded stability
stopping.
\item \code{DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 50000, backend = "auto")}: the
automatic deterministic policy, using cross-checked Genz-Sobol when supported
and deterministic FORTRAN otherwise.
\end{itemize}

The ``original randomized QMC'' row is included separately from the default
because the default may stop early once its tolerance is met.  The fixed-budget
row is often the cleaner comparison to \code{DeterministicQMC} because both are
asked to spend the same nominal number of points.  The Auto row is deliberately
given a larger budget because it splits the work across deterministic
cross-checks; this is a benchmark setting for \code{abseps = 1e-5}, not a
proposed package default.

\section{Benchmark design}

The examples below use equicorrelated-by-distance Toeplitz correlation
matrices, semi-infinite lower bounds, and finite upper bounds.  The dimensions
are deliberately modest so that package checks remain fast and so that the
\code{Miwa} comparison is still available.  Accuracy is measured as relative
error against a higher-step \code{Miwa(steps = 512)} calculation, to five
significant digits.  This reference is not a proof of the exact probability; it
is a stable deterministic yardstick for this small benchmark.

Each reported time is the median elapsed time over three evaluations on the
machine building the vignette.  Stochastic methods are run with a fixed seed so
that the values in the table are reproducible, although the timing itself is
necessarily machine dependent.

<<benchmark, echo=TRUE>>=
make_problem <- function(n) {
    list(corr = toeplitz(0.35^(0:(n - 1))),
         lower = rep(-Inf, n),
         upper = seq(-0.2, 0.6, length.out = n))
}

methods <- list(
    list(name = "Default GenzBretz",
         algorithm = GenzBretz(),
         seed = 11L),
    list(name = "Miwa steps=128",
         algorithm = Miwa(steps = 128),
         seed = NULL),
    list(name = "Original randomized QMC",
         algorithm = GenzBretz(maxpts = 25000, abseps = 0, releps = 0),
         seed = 11L),
    list(name = "DeterministicQMC C",
         algorithm = DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 25000, abseps = 0,
                                      releps = 0, backend = "c"),
         seed = NULL),
    list(name = "DeterministicQMC Sobol",
         algorithm = DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 25000, abseps = 0,
                                      releps = 0, backend = "sobol"),
         seed = NULL),
    list(name = "DeterministicQMC Hybrid",
         algorithm = DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 25000, abseps = 0,
                                      releps = 0, backend = "hybrid"),
         seed = NULL),
    list(name = "DeterministicQMC GenzSobol",
         algorithm = DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 25000, abseps = 1e-5,
                                      releps = 0, backend = "genzsobol"),
         seed = NULL),
    list(name = "DeterministicQMC Auto",
         algorithm = DeterministicQMC(maxpts = 50000, abseps = 1e-5,
                                      releps = 0, backend = "auto"),
         seed = NULL)
)

timed_value <- function(problem, method, reps = 3L) {
    times <- numeric(reps)
    value <- NA_real_
    diagnostic <- NA_real_
    target_requested <- is.list(method$algorithm) &&
        ((length(method$algorithm$abseps) == 1L &&
          !is.na(method$algorithm$abseps) && method$algorithm$abseps > 0) ||
         (length(method$algorithm$releps) == 1L &&
          !is.na(method$algorithm$releps) && method$algorithm$releps > 0))
    target_status <- "not requested"
    for (i in seq_len(reps)) {
        if (!is.null(method$seed))
            set.seed(method$seed)
        invisible(gc(FALSE))
        tm <- system.time({
            ans <- pmvnorm(lower = problem$lower, upper = problem$upper,
                           corr = problem$corr,
                           algorithm = method$algorithm)
        })
        times[i] <- unname(tm["elapsed"])
        if (i == 1L) {
            value <- as.numeric(ans)
            diagnostic <- attr(ans, "error")
            if (target_requested)
                target_status <- if (identical(attr(ans, "msg"),
                                               "Normal Completion"))
                    "met" else "not met"
        }
    }
    list(value = value, diagnostic = diagnostic,
         target_status = target_status, elapsed = median(times))
}

benchmark_dimension <- function(n) {
    problem <- make_problem(n)
    reference <- as.numeric(pmvnorm(lower = problem$lower,
                                    upper = problem$upper,
                                    corr = problem$corr,
                                    algorithm = Miwa(steps = 512)))
    rows <- lapply(methods, function(method) {
        ans <- timed_value(problem, method)
        data.frame(dimension = n,
                   method = method$name,
                   elapsed_sec = sprintf("%.4f", ans$elapsed),
                   value = sprintf("%.8g", ans$value),
                   diagnostic = sprintf("%.3e", ans$diagnostic),
                   target_status = ans$target_status,
                   rel_error = sprintf("%.5g",
                       if (reference == 0) NA_real_ else
                           abs(ans$value - reference) / abs(reference)),
                   stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
    })
    do.call(rbind, rows)
}

results <- do.call(rbind, lapply(c(4L, 8L, 12L), benchmark_dimension))
print(results, row.names = FALSE)
@

\section{Reading the results}

The default \code{GenzBretz()} row answers the practical question ``what happens
if I use \pkg{mvtnorm} as shipped?''  The fixed-budget QMC row answers a
different question: ``what does the original randomized QMC path do if it is
given the same nominal point budget as the deterministic C backend?''

For low dimensions, \code{Miwa} is often competitive and accurate.  Its
disadvantage is structural rather than visible in every small table: it is a
grid-based deterministic method and is limited to normal probabilities in
dimension at most 20.  Increasing the number of grid steps can improve accuracy
but increases cost.

\code{DeterministicQMC} is intended to occupy a different part of the design
space.  It gives a deterministic QMC-style calculation under \code{maxpts}
control.  This is useful when repeated evaluations must agree bit for bit
without managing seeds.  The Halton row is the simple first C backend.  The
Sobol row tests the first stronger deterministic point set in the same
transformed integral.  The Hybrid row keeps the Sobol backbone but applies a
deterministic tail-spaced warp with a Jacobian correction.  The trade-off is
that the C backends do not provide the mature randomized error assessment or
all adaptive refinements of \code{GenzBretz}.  Therefore the benchmark should be
read as an implementation baseline, not as a claim that the first C backends are
already uniformly faster than the stochastic default.  The GenzSobol row is
allowed to use its deterministic embedded stopping rule, so it answers a
slightly different speed question than the fixed-budget C, Sobol, and Hybrid
rows.  The Auto row adds deterministic cross-checks at fixed sequence offsets
and reports the larger of the embedded Genz-Sobol stability errors and the
between-check spread.  When a tolerance is requested, those individual checks
use a tighter internal target before the combined diagnostic is compared with
the requested \code{abseps}/\code{releps}.  It is therefore the closest row to
the unattended best-practice deterministic policy: somewhat more work is
acceptable in exchange for a more conservative reproducible accuracy
diagnostic.  The larger Auto budget in this benchmark compensates for those
repeated cross-checks; it should not be read as a change to the current package
defaults.

When \code{abseps} or \code{releps} is positive, \code{DeterministicQMC}
treats it as a request that the reported deterministic diagnostic meet the
requested target before \code{maxpts} is exhausted.  If the diagnostic is
larger than the requested tolerance, the returned object has \code{inform = 1}
and the usual message \code{"Completion with error > abseps"}.  In the table,
\code{target\_status} records whether such a target was requested and met.
This is a warning about the diagnostic, not a proof that the true integration
error is above or below the target.

\hypertarget{a-proposed-speed-target}{}
\section{Sobol, hybrid, Genz-Sobol}

The benchmark now includes three implementations of the proposed deterministic
Genz-Sobol direction.  \code{backend = "sobol"} keeps the sequential Genz
transformation and uses Sobol direction numbers with Gray-code point generation
in plain C.  \code{backend = "hybrid"} applies a gentle deterministic
tail-spaced change of variables to the Sobol coordinates, with the
corresponding Jacobian correction.  That makes the nonuniform-grid idea
testable without replacing QMC by a full tensor grid.
\code{backend = "genzsobol"} adds marginal-width variable reordering and
embedded powers-of-two stability checks using \code{abseps} and
\code{releps}.  \code{backend = "auto"} wraps that Genz-Sobol backend in a
deterministic selection policy: supported normal problems use several fixed
sequence offsets and unsupported cases use deterministic FORTRAN rather than
falling back to randomized QMC.

The remaining target is not just ``use Sobol'' or ``warp the tails''; it is to
add the surrounding algorithmic pieces that make the randomized Genz-Bretz path
so effective:

\begin{itemize}
\item keep the sequential Genz transformation and the same probability
interface;
\item reorder variables before integration to reduce effective dimension;
\item use a small pilot pass to identify conditional tail regions or
high-curvature coordinates before selecting any refinement grid;
\item replace the present fixed sequence offsets with stronger deterministic
digital shifts when a stability diagnostic is needed;
\item use fixed deterministic shifts only as a reproducible stability check,
not as randomization;
\item keep the FORTRAN deterministic path as a reference and fallback.
\end{itemize}

The fully developed version would aim at the speed of \code{GenzBretz} by
using fast point generation, embedded refinement, effective-dimension
reduction, and selective tail-aware refinement.  It would aim at the accuracy
of \code{GenzBretz} by retaining the Genz transformation and using a stronger
low-discrepancy rule than the current Halton prototype.  The important caveat
is that the error attribute would still be deterministic: it could measure
convergence and stability across fixed blocks, but it would not be a Monte
Carlo confidence statement.

\section{Accuracy caveats}

There are two different notions of uncertainty in this comparison.  The
\code{GenzBretz} error attribute is tied to randomized integration.  The
\code{DeterministicQMC} error attribute is a deterministic block-stability
diagnostic.  These quantities can be numerically useful, but they are not the
same statistical object.  For applications where stochastic error assessment is
important, \code{GenzBretz} remains the conservative default.  For deterministic
optimization, testing, or reproducible simulation pipelines, the new backend
provides a stable alternative.

Positive \code{abseps} and \code{releps} values request deterministic
diagnostic error control only.  They do not provide a Monte Carlo confidence
interval and they do not prove a rigorous deterministic error bound.

